\chapter{Graphs, Terms and Reduction}\label{ch37-graphs-terms-and-reduction}

\texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}hpp} and \texttt{MuDSL.\allowbreak{}d}
provide a directed multigraph, first-order terms, term rewriting, and
sharing-preserving term graph reduction. The feature tags are
\texttt{Graphs}, \texttt{Terms}, \texttt{TermRewriting}, and
\texttt{GraphReduction}. As with
\texttt{EqualitySaturati\allowbreak{}on}, the types and functions are
also usable independently of feature composition. C++ names live in
\texttt{dsl}; D names are module-level.

\section{Directed graphs}\label{ch37-directed-graphs}

In C++, construct
\texttt{DirectedGraph\textless{}\allowbreak{}NodeValue,\allowbreak{} EdgeValue\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}};
both payload types default to
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}string}. In D, use
\texttt{new DirectedGrap\allowbreak{}h!(\allowbreak{}NodeValue,\allowbreak{} EdgeValue)\allowbreak{}};
both default to \texttt{string}. D graphs are reference types. C++
graphs are value types. These mutable objects are not synchronized.

\begin{code}
DirectedGraph<> graph;
auto entry = graph.add_node("entry");
auto exit = graph.add_node("exit");
auto edge = graph.add_edge(entry, exit, "next");
auto path = graph.shortest_path(entry, exit);
assert(path && path->size() == 2);
std::string dot = graph.to_dot("Control flow");
\end{code}

\texttt{GraphNodeId} and \texttt{GraphEdgeId} are graph-local integer
handles. Handles are never recycled; removal invalidates only the
removed handles. Using an invalid handle throws. Handles from different
graphs must not be mixed. Parallel edges, self-loops, cycles, and
isolated nodes are supported. Edge insertion order is preserved in
adjacency lists, including when an incoming edge is redirected.

