Traversals, shortest paths, topological sorting, and strongly connected
components take linear time in visited vertices and edges, apart from
scanning retired storage slots for whole-graph operations. Node and edge
enumeration and counts scan the corresponding storage. Deletion removes
entries from adjacency arrays. References returned by C++ accessors can
be invalidated by subsequent graph mutations; retain IDs rather than
references across mutations.

\section{DOT output}\label{ch37-dot-output}

\texttt{to\_\allowbreak{}dot(\allowbreak{}name = "G")\allowbreak{}}
returns complete directed Graphviz DOT text, including isolated nodes
and parallel edges. Internal IDs such as \texttt{n0} are used as DOT
identifiers; payloads become quoted labels. Quotes, backslashes, and
line breaks are escaped. Tabs are retained as literal tabs. Other ASCII
control characters are rejected. Output order is deterministic for the
same mutation history.

The default renderer uses stream insertion in C++ and \texttt{to!string}
in D. For arbitrary payloads, pass label callbacks:

\begin{code}
auto dot = graph.to_dot(
    [](const std::string& value) { return "node: " + value; },
    [](const std::string& value) { return value; },
    "Annotated graph");
std::ofstream("graph.dot") << dot;
\end{code}

\begin{code}
import MuDSL;
import std.file : write;

auto graph = new DirectedGraph!();
auto entry = graph.add_node("entry");
auto exit = graph.add_node("exit");
graph.add_edge(entry, exit, "next");
write("graph.dot", graph.to_dot("Control flow"));
\end{code}

DOT generation has no external dependency. To turn the text into an
image, install Graphviz and run
\texttt{dot -Tsvg graph.\allowbreak{}dot -o graph.\allowbreak{}svg}. The
toolkit does not launch Graphviz or write files implicitly.

\section{First-order terms}\label{ch37-first-order-terms}

\texttt{Term} is a structural value with a \texttt{symbol}, ordered
\texttt{arguments}, and \texttt{is\_\allowbreak{}variable}. Variables
and nullary function symbols are distinct. Both implementations copy
terms deeply. D exposes \texttt{clone(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} for
copying a const term into a mutable value.

\begin{code}
auto variable = Term::variable("x");
auto zero = Term::function("0");
auto sum = Term::function("add", {variable, zero});
assert(sum == parse_term("(add ?x 0)"));
\end{code}

D uses
\texttt{Term.\allowbreak{}variable(\allowbreak{}"x")\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{Term.\allowbreak{}function\_\allowbreak{}(\allowbreak{}"add",\allowbreak{} arguments)\allowbreak{}}
because \texttt{function} is a D keyword.
\texttt{parse\_\allowbreak{}term} uses the existing S-expression reader.
Unquoted \texttt{?name} atoms are variables; quoted \texttt{"?name"}
atoms are literal symbols. Function heads must be literal atoms. Empty
lists, variable heads, empty variable names, and variables with
arguments are rejected. Symbols may contain spaces or punctuation;
dumping quotes them as needed. There is no global signature table: the
same function name can appear at different arities, but matching and
unification require both name and arity to agree.

\texttt{dump(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} and
\texttt{sexpr(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} serialize terms;
\texttt{term\_\allowbreak{}from\_\allowbreak{}sexpr(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
converts the other way. \texttt{variables(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}}
returns a set of bare variable names (a \texttt{bool[string]} map in D),
\texttt{ground(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} tests for absence of
variables, and \texttt{size(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} counts tree
nodes. \texttt{at(\allowbreak{}position)\allowbreak{}} selects a subterm
using zero-based argument indices; the empty position selects the root.
\texttt{replaced(\allowbreak{}position,\allowbreak{} replacement)\allowbreak{}}
returns a new term and leaves the original unchanged. Invalid positions
throw.

\texttt{TermSubstitution\allowbreak{}} maps bare variable names to
terms.
\texttt{match\_\allowbreak{}term(\allowbreak{}pattern,\allowbreak{} subject)\allowbreak{}}
returns optional bindings and enforces structural equality at repeated
variables. Subject variables are treated as subject data, not as
additional pattern variables. A failed match publishes no partial
bindings.

\texttt{substitute(\allowbreak{}term,\allowbreak{} bindings)\allowbreak{}}
performs simultaneous substitution; absent variables remain unchanged
and inserted bindings are not recursively substituted again.
\texttt{unify\_\allowbreak{}terms(\allowbreak{}left,\allowbreak{} right)\allowbreak{}}
returns an optional most general unifier with an occurs check. Its
bindings are composed so one simultaneous substitution makes both input
terms equal. It rejects cyclic equations such as
\texttt{?x = (\allowbreak{}f ?x)\allowbreak{}}. Optional results use
\texttt{std:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}optional} in C++ and
\texttt{Nullable} (\texttt{isNull} / \texttt{get}) in D.

\section{Term rewrite systems}\label{ch37-term-rewrite-systems}

\texttt{TermRule} contains \texttt{name}, \texttt{lhs}, and
\texttt{rhs}. Rules require a nonvariable left side and bind every
right-side variable on the left. \texttt{TermRewriteSyste\allowbreak{}m}
validates and owns copies of its rules. \texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}rule}
accepts a \texttt{TermRule} or an S-expression string;
\texttt{term\_\allowbreak{}rule} parses a standalone rule. Supported
forms are
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}rewrite name lhs\allowbreak{} rhs)\allowbreak{}},
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}= lhs rhs)\allowbreak{}}, and
\texttt{(\allowbreak{}=\textgreater{}\allowbreak{} lhs rhs)\allowbreak{}}.

\begin{code}
TermRewriteSystem rules;
rules.add_rule("(rewrite add-zero (add ?x 0) ?x)");
rules.add_rule("(rewrite mul-one (mul ?x 1) ?x)");
auto report = rules.normalize(parse_term("(mul (add a 0) 1)"), 100);
assert(report.normal_form() && report.steps == 2);
assert(report.term == parse_term("a"));
\end{code}

\texttt{rewrite\_\allowbreak{}once(\allowbreak{}term,\allowbreak{} strategy)\allowbreak{}}
returns an optional \texttt{TermRewriteStep} containing the entire
rewritten \texttt{term}, \texttt{rule\_\allowbreak{}index}, and redex
\texttt{position}. Rules are tried in insertion order at each position.
\texttt{RewriteStrategy:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}Outermost} searches
root before children; \texttt{Innermost} searches children before root.
Both visit arguments from left to right. D uses enum access with
\texttt{.\allowbreak{}} instead of
\texttt{:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}}.

\texttt{normalize(\allowbreak{}term,\allowbreak{} max\_\allowbreak{}steps = 1000,\allowbreak{} strategy = Oute\allowbreak{}rmost)\allowbreak{}}
returns a \texttt{TermReductionRes\allowbreak{}ult} with \texttt{term},
\texttt{steps}, and \texttt{stop}.
\texttt{ReductionStop:\allowbreak{}:\allowbreak{}NormalForm} means no
rule matches anywhere; \texttt{StepLimit} means another rewrite is
available but the budget is exhausted. A zero budget only checks
reducibility. A term that reaches normal form on the last allowed step
reports \texttt{NormalForm}. Identity rules count as rewrites and
exhaust the budget rather than being silently discarded. Termination and
confluence are not assumed or proved.

\section{Shared term graphs and graph
reduction}\label{ch37-shared-term-graphs-and-graph-reduction}

\texttt{TermGraph} stores ordered, acyclic term graphs using
\texttt{DirectedGraph\textless{}\allowbreak{}TermGraphLabel,\allowbreak{} size\_\allowbreak{}t\textgreater{}\allowbreak{}}.
Its graph view is read-only so the term shape and acyclicity invariants
cannot be broken by ordinary callers. Nodes carry a symbol and variable
flag; edges carry zero-based argument indices.

\begin{code}
TermRewriteSystem rules;
rules.add_rule("(= (add ?x 0) ?x)");
TermGraph graph;
auto root = graph.add("(pair (add a 0) (add a 0))");
auto report = graph.reduce(root, rules, 100);
assert(report.normal_form() && report.steps == 1);
assert(graph.to_term(report.root) == parse_term("(pair a a)"));
graph.collect_garbage({report.root});
std::ofstream("reduced.dot") << graph.to_dot();
\end{code}

\begin{code}
auto rules = new TermRewriteSystem;
rules.add_rule("(= (add ?x 0) ?x)");
auto graph = new TermGraph;
auto root = graph.add("(pair (add a 0) (add a 0))");
auto report = graph.reduce(root, rules, 100);
assert(report.normal_form() && report.steps == 1);
assert(graph.to_term(report.root) == parse_term("(pair a a)"));
graph.collect_garbage([report.root]);
\end{code}

\texttt{add(\allowbreak{}term\_\allowbreak{}or\_\allowbreak{}source,\allowbreak{} share = true)\allowbreak{}}
reuses existing nodes with equal labels and identical child handles.
With \texttt{share = false}, each occurrence gets its own nodes.
\texttt{add\_\allowbreak{}node(\allowbreak{}symbol,\allowbreak{} children)\allowbreak{}}
explicitly constructs a function node with existing children and never
merges it. These construction operations cannot introduce cycles.
\texttt{graph(\allowbreak{})\allowbreak{}} exposes traversal and read
APIs; \texttt{to\_\allowbreak{}term(\allowbreak{}root)\allowbreak{}}
unfolds a graph into a tree;
\texttt{to\_\allowbreak{}dot(\allowbreak{}name = "TermGrap\allowbreak{}h")\allowbreak{}}
renders all live nodes. Each sharing-enabled \texttt{add} builds a hash
index of live nodes and interns the input bottom-up. Rebuilding the
index keeps it consistent with reductions and edge redirections between
additions.

Graph matching operates directly on node handles. Repeated variables
compare subgraphs structurally even when they are not physically shared.
When a rule fires, right-side variables reuse their bound nodes. Every
incoming edge of the redex is redirected to the replacement, so all
occurrences of a shared redex change in one reduction. The redex node is
removed. Newly introduced function nodes are fresh; rewriting does not
automatically maximize sharing.

\texttt{rewrite\_\allowbreak{}once(\allowbreak{}root,\allowbreak{} rules,\allowbreak{} strategy)\allowbreak{}}
returns an optional \texttt{GraphRewriteStep\allowbreak{}} with
\texttt{root}, \texttt{redex}, \texttt{replacement}, and
\texttt{rule\_\allowbreak{}index}.
\texttt{reduce(\allowbreak{}root,\allowbreak{} rules,\allowbreak{} max\_\allowbreak{}steps = 1000,\allowbreak{} strategy = Oute\allowbreak{}rmost)\allowbreak{}}
returns \texttt{GraphReductionRe\allowbreak{}sult} with \texttt{root},
\texttt{steps}, and \texttt{stop}. Only nodes reachable from the
selected root are searched. The same rule priority, strategy, and budget
contracts apply as for term rewriting, with each shared node visited
once per search.

Always continue with the returned root: rewriting the root removes its
old ID. Other external root handles that equal a removed redex must also
be updated using the step's replacement. Incoming edges from other roots
are redirected too. Garbage is retained until
\texttt{collect\_\allowbreak{}garbage(\allowbreak{}roots)\allowbreak{}};
supply every root you want to keep. Empty roots remove all nodes.
Invalid roots throw before collection removes anything. Remaining
handles stay stable.

The general graph supports cycles; \texttt{TermGraph} deliberately
represents finite acyclic terms. Step budgets limit rewrites, not memory
or recursion depth. Tree operations recurse through terms, and unfolding
a highly shared graph can produce an exponentially larger tree. These
APIs do not implement higher-order binding, cyclic term reduction,
completion, or equality saturation; the separate \texttt{EGraph} API
remains available for equality saturation.

\section{Verification}\label{ch37-verification}

The graph and term regression tests cover both implementations. The
standalone test project includes D tests automatically when
\texttt{ldc2}, \texttt{dmd}, or \texttt{gdc} is available; use
\texttt{-DMuDSL\_\allowbreak{}TEST\_\allowbreak{}D=OFF} to disable that
target.

\begin{code}
cmake -S tests -B build/tests -DCMAKE_BUILD_TYPE=Debug
cmake --build build/tests
ctest --test-dir build/tests --output-on-failure
ldc2 -unittest -main MuDSL.d tests/test_graph_terms.d -of=mudsl-tests
./mudsl-tests
\end{code}
